-- title:
--   Theorem 3.3(1), p. 7 — ℙ_x(T_y < ζ) = (H(x) − H^[y(x))/H(y) (part 1 of 4; companions theorem_3_3_2, _3, _4)
-- statement:
--   Let $E=[\mathfrak l,\mathfrak h]\cap\mathbb Z$ and let $(X,\mathbb P)$ be the Markov chain with state space $E$ generated by a Q-matrix $Q$ (not necessarily conservative) that is skip-free downward, i.e. satisfies (2.1): $Q(x,x-1)>0$ for $x>\mathfrak l$ and $Q(x,y)=0$ for $y\le x-2$. Assume all states are transient and fix a reference point $\mathfrak o\in E$. Write $G$ for the resolvent, $\zeta$ for the lifetime and $T_A$ for the first hitting time of $A$. Let $H$, $\widehat H$, $\mathbf H^{[b}$ and $\mathfrak C$ be as in Theorem 3.1.
--
--   Then for all $x,y\in E$
--   $$\mathbb P_x(T_y<\zeta)=\frac{H(x)-\mathbf H^{[y}(x)}{H(y)}.\tag{3.6}$$
--
--   This gives the hitting probability of a single state from either side.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: E = [𝔩, 𝔥] ∩ ℤ with 𝔩 < 𝔥 in ℤ ∪ {±∞} → E : Set ℤ with E.OrdConnected and two points x < y in E (𝔩 = −∞ iff ¬ BddBelow E, 𝔥 = ∞ iff ¬ BddAbove E); reason: an order-connected subset of ℤ with at least two points is exactly such an integer interval
--   - encoding: all states in E are transient → AllTransient E Q: G(x, x) < ∞ for every x ∈ E; reason: Norris, Markov Chains, Theorem 3.4.2, which the paper itself invokes on p. 3; an absorbing state has G(x, x) = ∞
--   - encoding: the Markov chain (X, ℙ) generated by Q (jump chain/holding time definition, p. 2) → transition, resolvent, hitProb, hitBeforeProb, exitDist, killedResolvent computed from jump-chain path sums with Exp(1) holding clocks; reason: this is the paper's construction (i)–(iv); the cemetery ∂ is represented by leaving E
--   - encoding: real-valued probabilities ℙ_x(…) → ℝ≥0∞-valued path sums compared through .toReal; reason: a probability is at most 1, so .toReal loses nothing
--   - reading: the notation of Theorem 3.1: H(x) and H^{[y}(x) = lim_{y↓𝔩} … (no printed case split for H^{[b}) → fundH, fundHb: value at 𝔩 when E is bounded below, limUnder along atBot otherwise; existence of these limits is asserted in theorem_3_1_1 and theorem_3_1_3; reason: the paper defines the notation lim_{y↓𝔩} by this case split for H in Theorem 3.1(1); Theorem 3.3 is stated 'using its notation'
-- source:
--   Loeffen, Patie, Wang, Fluctuation Theory of Continuous-Time, Skip-Free Downward Markov Chains with Applications to Branching Processes with Immigration, arXiv:2208.14425v1, p. 7, Theorem 3.3(1), (3.6)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SkipFreeFluct_MBI_Chain
open scoped ENNReal Topology
open Filter MeasureTheory

namespace SkipFreeFluct.MBI

/-- Theorem 3.3(1), p. 7, (3.6). Under the assumptions of Theorem 3.1, for `x, y ∈ E`:
`ℙ_x(T_y < ζ) = (H(x) - H^{[y}(x))/H(y)`. -/
theorem theorem_3_3_1 (E : Set ℤ) (Q : ℤ → ℤ → ℝ) (hE : IsStateSpace E) (hQ : IsQMatrix E Q)
    (hS : SkipFreeDown E Q) (hT : AllTransient E Q) (o : ℤ) (ho : o ∈ E) :
    ∀ x ∈ E, ∀ y ∈ E,
      (hitProb E Q {y} x).toReal = (fundH E Q o x - fundHb E Q o y x) / fundH E Q o y := by sorry

end SkipFreeFluct.MBI
